{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over temple roofs. I spent my first morning sipping matcha in a quiet teahouse, listening to the rustle of bamboo and the distant chime of a temple bell. No rush. No noise. Just stillness.\n\nBy afternoon, I wandered Nishiki Market, where vendors offered glistening tuna slices, pickled plums, and matcha mochi so sweet it made me smile. I tried takoyaki for the first time—crispy outside, molten inside—and nearly burned my tongue. Worth it.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed with neon and precision. Shibuya’s scramble crossing was a symphony of movement; I stood frozen, overwhelmed and enchanted. I rode the bullet train to Hakone, where Mount Fuji rose like a snow-capped guardian above misty lakes. I soaked in an onsen at dusk, steam curling around me as stars blinked awake.\n\nWhat surprised me most? The kindness. A shopkeeper patiently helped me pick out a hand-painted fan, speaking slowly in broken English. An elderly woman bowed deeply when I thanked her for directions. Even in crowds, there was quiet respect—a culture of care woven into every gesture.\n\nI bought a small wooden fox charm from Fushimi Inari, its red torii gates stretching endlessly up the hillside like a river of fire. I didn’t know then that foxes are messengers of the gods in Shinto belief. Now, I keep it on my desk. A reminder that wonder lives in small things: a perfect cup of tea, a stranger’s smile, the silence between raindrops.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me beauty—it taught me how to see it.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.22264541685581207}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.060953639447689056}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31330111622810364}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.005334